---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'YTCPM Bot — Installation and Setup Guide';
const description =
	'Beginner-friendly installation and setup guide for YTCPM Bot: download, extract, first launch, login, every settings tab, timing, troubleshooting, and responsible use.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
		<style>
			/* Instructions page only — uses the site's existing design tokens.
			   No global styles are changed. */
			.eyebrow {
				font-size: 0.75em;
				font-weight: 700;
				letter-spacing: 0.12em;
				text-transform: uppercase;
				color: var(--accent);
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
			}
			.lead {
				font-size: 1.15em;
			}
			section {
				margin-bottom: 2.5em;
			}
			.card {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-radius: 12px;
				padding: 1em 1.1em;
				background: #fff;
				margin-top: 1em;
			}
			.card h3 {
				font-size: 1.1em;
				margin-bottom: 0.4em;
			}
			.note {
				border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 35%);
				border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
				margin-top: 1em;
			}
			.note p {
				margin: 0;
			}
			.warn {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-left: 4px solid #b91c1c;
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 25%);
				border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
				margin-top: 1em;
			}
			.warn p {
				margin: 0;
			}
			table.info {
				width: 100%;
				border-collapse: collapse;
				font-size: 0.9em;
				margin-top: 1em;
			}
			table.info th,
			table.info td {
				text-align: left;
				padding: 0.5em 0.75em;
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				vertical-align: top;
			}
			table.info th {
				width: 34%;
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 35%);
				color: rgb(var(--black));
			}
			table.info td.shown {
				width: 22%;
				white-space: nowrap;
			}
			.toc {
				border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
				border-radius: 12px;
				padding: 1em 1.2em;
				background: rgba(var(--gray-light), 25%);
			}
			.toc ol {
				margin: 0;
			}
			.actions {
				display: flex;
				gap: 0.75em;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
				margin-top: 1.25em;
			}
			.btn {
				display: inline-block;
				padding: 0.45em 1.1em;
				border-radius: 999px;
				font-weight: 700;
				font-size: 0.85em;
				text-decoration: none;
				border: 2px solid var(--accent);
			}
			.btn-primary {
				background: var(--accent);
				color: #fff;
			}
			.btn-primary:hover {
				color: #fff;
				background: var(--accent-dark);
				border-color: var(--accent-dark);
			}
			.btn-ghost {
				color: var(--accent);
				background: transparent;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section aria-labelledby="guide-heading">
				<p class="eyebrow">Setup Guide</p>
				<h1 id="guide-heading">YTCPM Bot — Installation and Setup Guide</h1>
				<p class="lead">
					This beginner-friendly guide takes you from download to a running bot: what to
					download, how to extract it, what happens on first launch, how signing in works, and
					what every setting in the bot means.
				</p>
				<div class="toc">
					<strong>On this page</strong>
					<ol>
						<li><a href="#step-1">Download the bot</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-2">Extract the ZIP file</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-3">Do you need Browser Automation Studio?</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-4">Configure the bot (every settings tab)</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-5">First-time startup and sign-in</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-6">How long does the first run take?</a></li>
						<li><a href="#step-7">Run the bot and understand results</a></li>
						<li><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a></li>
						<li><a href="#security">Security and responsible use</a></li>
					</ol>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 1 -->
			<section id="step-1" aria-labelledby="step-1-heading">
				<h2 id="step-1-heading">Step 1: Download the Bot</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Go to the <a href="/download">download page</a>.</li>
					<li>
						Click <strong>Download YTCPM Bot</strong>. Your browser downloads
						<code>YoutubeCPM.zip</code> (17.0&nbsp;MB).
					</li>
					<li>
						If the browser asks where to save it, choose a folder you can find again — the
						Windows <strong>Downloads</strong> folder is fine.
					</li>
					<li>Wait until the download finishes completely before continuing.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>
					<strong>Finding the file afterwards:</strong> press <code>Ctrl + J</code> in your
					browser to open its Downloads list, or open File Explorer and click
					<strong>Downloads</strong> in the left sidebar. Look for <code>YoutubeCPM.zip</code>.
				</p>
				<div class="note">
					<p>
						<strong>Optional check:</strong> verify the file is genuine and complete. In the
						Downloads folder, run
						<code>certutil -hashfile YoutubeCPM.zip SHA256</code> in a terminal and compare it
						with the SHA-256 checksum published on the <a href="/download">download page</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 2 -->
			<section id="step-2" aria-labelledby="step-2-heading">
				<h2 id="step-2-heading">Step 2: Extract the ZIP File</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Locate <code>YoutubeCPM.zip</code> in your Downloads folder.</li>
					<li>Right-click the archive and select <strong>Extract All…</strong></li>
					<li>Choose a destination folder (the suggested folder is fine) and click <strong>Extract</strong>.</li>
					<li>Open the extracted folder.</li>
					<li>
						Go into the <strong><code>YoutubeCPM</code></strong> folder inside it — that is
						where the application lives.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p>After extraction you should see this structure:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<code>YoutubeCPM</code> folder containing <code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code>, its library
						(.dll) files, and subfolders such as <code>data</code>, <code>platforms</code>, and
						<code>translations</code>.
					</li>
					<li>
						Next to that folder: <code>List_of_Refrences.txt</code>,
						<code>User_Agents.txt</code>, and <code>proxy.txt</code>.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<div class="warn">
					<p>
						<strong>Do not run the bot from inside the ZIP archive.</strong> Always extract
						first, and keep the folder structure exactly as extracted — do not move
						<code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code> away from its .dll files, and do not delete the three
						text files, because the bot's settings point to them.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 3 -->
			<section id="step-3" aria-labelledby="step-3-heading">
				<h2 id="step-3-heading">Step 3: Do You Need Browser Automation Studio?</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>No — not to run this package.</strong> YTCPM Bot was built with Browser
					Automation Studio (BAS, by Bablosoft) and is distributed as a standalone, compiled
					application. There is no BAS project file to import and no BAS menu to open: you
					simply run <code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code> (Step 5), and its script launcher downloads
					the BAS engine it needs by itself.
				</p>
				<p>
					If you want Browser Automation Studio itself — for example, to learn how BAS bots
					are built — it is a free download from the official Bablosoft website:
					<a href="https://bablosoft.com/shop/BrowserAutomationStudio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
						bablosoft.com/shop/BrowserAutomationStudio
					</a>. It is not part of this package and is not required for anything in this guide.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 4 -->
			<section id="step-4" aria-labelledby="step-4-heading">
				<h2 id="step-4-heading">Step 4: Configure the Bot</h2>
				<p>
					When the bot starts, it opens a settings window titled
					<strong>Please define resources</strong>. Work through the tabs below, then click
					<strong>OK</strong> to continue (or <strong>Cancel</strong> to stop). The values in
					the “Shown as” column are the values from the reference screenshots — treat them as
					starting points, not recommendations.
				</p>

				<article class="card">
					<h3>General</h3>
					<table class="info">
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Number of Browsers</th>
								<td>How many browser instances the bot runs in parallel. More browsers do more work at once, but use more CPU, memory, and bandwidth.</td>
								<td class="shown">10</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Sleep between every page load, in seconds</th>
								<td>A random pause between the minimum and maximum values, applied between page loads.</td>
								<td class="shown">Min 3 · Max 5</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">List of Refrences Websites</th>
								<td>Select the <code>List_of_Refrences.txt</code> file that came with the package (use the folder button to browse to it).</td>
								<td class="shown">Bundled file</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Total Views</th>
								<td>The total number of views the workflow is set to work towards.</td>
								<td class="shown">9999999</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</article>

				<article class="card">
					<h3>Youtube Setting</h3>
					<table class="info">
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Enable Skip Ads</th>
								<td>Whether the bot skips ads when YouTube offers a skip option (<em>Yes</em> / <em>No</em>).</td>
								<td class="shown">Yes</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</article>

				<article class="card">
					<h3>Ads Setting</h3>
					<table class="info">
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Enable Ads</th>
								<td>Turn ads handling in the workflow on or off (<em>Yes</em> / <em>No</em>).</td>
								<td class="shown">Yes</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Select Ads Type</th>
								<td>Which ads type the workflow works with.</td>
								<td class="shown">DisplayAds</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Enable Scroll 4 Ads</th>
								<td>Whether the page is scrolled for ads (<em>Yes</em> / <em>No</em>).</td>
								<td class="shown">Yes</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Open Ads Internal page</th>
								<td>Whether the ads internal (landing) page is opened (<em>Yes</em> / <em>No</em>).</td>
								<td class="shown">Yes</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Time Duration for Ads, in Seconds</th>
								<td>A random duration between the minimum and maximum values for how long ads are shown.</td>
								<td class="shown">Min 30 · Max 50</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</article>

				<article class="card">
					<h3>Browser</h3>
					<table class="info">
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Enable Fingerprint, Premium Service</th>
								<td>
									Optional premium fingerprint service. Leave on <em>No</em> unless you have
									that service — the text field next to it is for the details the service
									requires when enabled.
								</td>
								<td class="shown">No</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Select User Agents</th>
								<td>How user agents are chosen. <em>List Mix</em> uses the list file below.</td>
								<td class="shown">List Mix</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">User Agents File Mix</th>
								<td>Select the <code>User_Agents.txt</code> file that came with the package (999 user-agent strings).</td>
								<td class="shown">Bundled file</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</article>

				<article class="card">
					<h3>Proxies</h3>
					<table class="info">
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Want to Used Proxies?</th>
								<td>Turn proxy use on or off (<em>Yes</em> / <em>No</em>). If you choose <em>No</em>, the rest of this tab is not needed.</td>
								<td class="shown">Yes</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Proxy Methods</th>
								<td>How proxies are supplied.</td>
								<td class="shown">Proxies</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">List of Proxies</th>
								<td>
									<strong>Required when proxies are enabled.</strong> Select your proxy list
									file — the package includes an empty <code>proxy.txt</code> for your own
									proxies (add them in the file, one entry per line, in the format your proxy
									provider supplies). A red <strong>!</strong> beside this tab means this
									field is missing or invalid.
								</td>
								<td class="shown">File required</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Proxy Type. Can be auto.</th>
								<td><em>auto</em> detects the type; or force <em>http</em> or <em>socks5</em>.</td>
								<td class="shown">auto</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Proxy Login. Can be blank.</th>
								<td>Optional. Only if your proxies require authentication.</td>
								<td class="shown">Blank</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<th scope="row">Proxy Password. Can be blank</th>
								<td>Optional. Only if your proxies require authentication. Never share it publicly.</td>
								<td class="shown">Blank</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</article>

				<h3>Required vs optional — at a glance</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Required:</strong> the User ID and password for sign-in (Step 5); the
						<code>List_of_Refrences.txt</code> and <code>User_Agents.txt</code> files (they
						come with the package — just keep them selected); and, if proxies are enabled, a
						List of Proxies file.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Optional:</strong> proxies in general, proxy login/password, and the
						premium fingerprint service.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<div class="note">
					<p>
						Never type your bot password, proxy password, or any other credential into a
						website, chat, or public place — credentials belong only in the bot's own windows.
						This page deliberately shows no real credentials.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 5 -->
			<section id="step-5" aria-labelledby="step-5-heading">
				<h2 id="step-5-heading">Step 5: First-Time Startup and Sign-In</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>
						Open the extracted <code>YoutubeCPM</code> folder and double-click
						<strong><code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code></strong>.
					</li>
					<li>
						The <strong>Script launcher</strong> window appears: “Downloading engine — The
						script will start automatically when it is ready.” On first run it downloads
						about <strong>1–1.5&nbsp;GB</strong> of engine files in the background.
						<strong>Wait for it to finish — do not close the launcher.</strong> You can watch
						the progress bar and percentage; click <em>Cancel</em> only if you want to stop.
					</li>
					<li>
						When the download completes, the script starts by itself and asks for your
						<strong>User ID</strong> and <strong>password</strong>.
					</li>
					<li>
						Enter the credentials provided with your access. If you want the bot to remember
						them on this computer, tick the <strong>Save</strong> option.
					</li>
					<li>
						Click <strong>OK</strong>. The bot checks the username and password — if they are
						correct, the main window opens. If they are rejected, re-check for typing errors
						and try again.
					</li>
					<li>
						Complete the settings window (<a href="#step-4">Step 4</a>) and click
						<strong>OK</strong> to run.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<h3>One-time vs every time</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>First run only:</strong> the 1–1.5&nbsp;GB engine download, and (if you
						ticked Save) storing your sign-in.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Every run:</strong> launch <code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code>, sign in (if not
						saved), confirm the settings, click OK.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 6 -->
			<section id="step-6" aria-labelledby="step-6-heading">
				<h2 id="step-6-heading">Step 6: How Long Does the First Run Take?</h2>
				<p>
					These are planning estimates, not guarantees — your connection and computer decide
					the real times:
				</p>
				<table class="info">
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Downloading the package</th>
							<td>About 1–5 minutes on typical broadband — the archive is only 17.0&nbsp;MB.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Extracting the ZIP</th>
							<td>About 1–3 minutes.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Installing BAS</th>
							<td>Not needed — there is nothing to install (see Step 3).</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">First-run engine download</th>
							<td>
								The big one: about 1–1.5&nbsp;GB downloads in the background after you first
								run <code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code>. On a fast connection this can be a few
								minutes; on a slow connection (around 10&nbsp;Mbps) it can take 15–25
								minutes or longer.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Initial configuration</th>
							<td>About 3–10 minutes if your sign-in details and any proxy list are ready.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">First execution</th>
							<td>Varies with the workflow, browser startup, network speed, and the settings you chose (browsers, total views, delays).</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p>
					Altogether, a straightforward setup takes around <strong>10–30 minutes</strong>,
					excluding a slow first-run engine download, which can extend the first launch
					beyond that. Later launches are faster: the engine is already downloaded and your
					sign-in and settings do not need to be set up again.
				</p>
				<div class="note">
					<p>
						There is no guaranteed execution or completion time for a run — it depends on
						your settings (for example, number of browsers and total views) and network
						conditions.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 7 -->
			<section id="step-7" aria-labelledby="step-7-heading">
				<h2 id="step-7-heading">Step 7: Run the Bot and Understand Results</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Launch <code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code> and sign in if asked (Step 5).</li>
					<li>
						In the <strong>Please define resources</strong> window, review each tab
						(<a href="#step-4">Step 4</a>) — especially the number of browsers, total views,
						and, if enabled, that a valid List of Proxies file is selected (no red
						<strong>!</strong>).
					</li>
					<li>Click <strong>OK</strong> to start the workflow.</li>
					<li>
						Monitor progress in the bot's window while it works. The browsers it opens are
						part of the workflow — let it run, and do not close the bot window mid-run.
					</li>
					<li>
						A run is finished when the bot stops working and returns to its idle/finished
						state in the main window. How long that takes depends on your settings — there
						is no fixed duration.
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p>
					The current package does not document a fixed output file, log file name, or results
					format in its files, so treat anything the bot shows in its own window as the result
					of the run, and check the bot window first if you are looking for status or output.
					If a future script version adds exported results, this section will be updated with
					the real file names.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Step 8 -->
			<section id="troubleshooting" aria-labelledby="troubleshooting-heading">
				<h2 id="troubleshooting-heading">Step 8: Troubleshooting</h2>

				<h3>Download interrupted or the archive is missing</h3>
				<p>
					Re-download from the <a href="/download">download page</a> and wait for it to finish
					completely. Check the file size (17.0&nbsp;MB) or the SHA-256 checksum shown there.
					If your browser removed it after a security scan, download it again from this site
					only — never from a re-upload on another site.
				</p>

				<h3>ZIP extraction errors</h3>
				<p>
					Usually a incomplete download or not enough free disk space. Delete the partial
					extraction, make sure you have at least 2&nbsp;GB free, re-download the archive, and
					extract again with <em>Extract All</em>.
				</p>

				<h3>“Do I need to install BAS?” / BAS missing or incompatible</h3>
				<p>
					You do not need BAS installed to run this package — it is a standalone application
					(Step 3). If the script launcher reports an engine problem, let it finish or retry
					its own engine download on a stable connection rather than installing anything
					manually.
				</p>

				<h3>The bot does not start, or the project file is not recognised</h3>
				<p>
					Make sure you extracted the ZIP first (Step 2) and are double-clicking
					<code>YoutubeCPM.exe</code> inside the extracted <code>YoutubeCPM</code> folder —
					not opening files from inside the archive, and not moving the .exe away from its
					.dll files. If <code>data/project.xml</code> is missing, the extraction is
					incomplete: extract a fresh copy.
				</p>

				<h3>Red ! next to a settings tab / required settings missing</h3>
				<p>
					A red <strong>!</strong> means a required resource is missing or invalid. The most
					common case: Proxies are enabled but no List of Proxies file is selected. Select
					your proxy list file (or set <em>Want to Used Proxies?</em> to <em>No</em>), and
					check that the References and User Agents files are still selected.
				</p>

				<h3>Sign-in is rejected</h3>
				<p>
					Re-type the User ID and password carefully (watch for extra spaces and Caps Lock).
					The bot verifies them before opening the main window, so correct credentials are
					required every run unless you used the <em>Save</em> option on this computer.
				</p>

				<h3>Browser startup or network errors; engine download fails</h3>
				<p>
					Check that you are online and that the connection is stable — the launcher needs to
					download 1–1.5&nbsp;GB on first run and reports download errors with an HTTP
					status. Close other heavy downloads, retry, and let the launcher run undisturbed.
					If browsers fail to start, restart the bot and lower the Number of Browsers.
				</p>

				<h3>The automation runs but produces no results</h3>
				<p>
					Check your settings: total views, the delays, and whether the References and User
					Agents files are valid. If proxies are enabled, test that your proxy list works —
					dead proxies are a common cause. There is no guaranteed outcome for any run.
